 ## Overview
-Implicit Surface Primitives represent 3D geometry as the zero-level set (or isosurface) of a scalar field function $f(\vec{x})$. Unlike explicit representations that store discrete topological data, this family defines geometry analytically, allowing for on-demand evaluation of values and gradients at arbitrary points in space. This approach enables the creation of complex, organic, or mathematically precise shapes that can be composed, deformed, or sampled for volume rendering and surface extraction.
+Implicit Surface Primitives represent 3D geometry as the zero-level set (or isosurface) of a scalar field function $f(\vec{x})$ [S1, unofficial]. Unlike explicit representations that store discrete topological data, this family defines geometry analytically, allowing for on-demand evaluation of values and gradients at arbitrary points in space [G]. This approach enables the creation of complex, organic, or mathematically precise shapes that can be composed, deformed, or sampled for volume rendering and surface extraction [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family rely on the evaluation of a scalar field function $f$ over a domain $\Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3$. The core mechanism identifies the surface as the locus of points where the function output equals a specific constant, typically zero.
+All methods in this family rely on the evaluation of a scalar field function $f$ over a domain $\Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3$ [Implicit Surface Definition](2798-implicit-surface-definition.md). The core mechanism identifies the surface as the locus of points where the function output equals a specific constant, typically zero [Implicit Surface Definition](2798-implicit-surface-definition.md).
 
 **Mathematical Definition:**
 The implicit surface is defined as the set of points $\vec{x}$ satisfying:
 $$ \{\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3 \mid f(\vec{x}) = 0\} $$
-
 More generally, an isosurface at isovalue $v$ is defined by:
 $$ f(\vec{x}) - v = 0 $$
